00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
at the height of an elephant poaching  crisis in the 1980s richard leakey  organized a spectacular publicity stunt  as director of kenya's wildlife  authority he authorized 12 tons of  confiscated elephant tusks worth  millions of dollars to be burnt  although the practice was widely copied  globally critics said the ivory should  have been sold to fund anti-perching  efforts we can burn ivory and do all  these things but unless we can persuade  the public why we're doing it  we'll be wasting our time  ivory must be seen as only valuable if  it's on an animal  it should not be ever seen as a valuable  commodity for trade  but his implementation of a shoot to  kill policy against armed pushers made  him powerful enemies  he lost both legs when the plane he was  flying crashed in the rift valley in  what he later said was a failed attempt  to kill him  leaky's parents were the world's most  famous finers of ancient hermeneut  fossils he continued their work and it  was in a dig in kenya's turkana region  in 1975 that leaky's team discovered an  almost complete skeleton of an early  human known as homo erectus  the find helped prove humans evolved in  africa it spurred a lifelong campaign to  preserve the turkana region  it's a natural heritage site with  evidence of prehistory not just of the  ancestry of humanity but the ancestry of  the african fauna is better represented  there than anywhere in the world  he was less successful when he tried his  hand at politics in 1998 he resigned  after just two years from his position  as the head of kenya's civil service he  had attempted a crackdown on corruption  richard leakey was 77.
 

